# Weekly LAT-Early Quit Extraction Script

## Instructions
1. Upload both files:
   - The Excel workbook 
   - The PDF attendance report 
2. Copy and paste the prompt below

---

## Prompt

Create a worksheet named with the PDF report run date in M-D-YY format (example: 4-20-26).

Parse the attached Weekly Full Attendance Report PDF (PE1-400 Absence Report).

Determine the report run date from the PDF header using THIS RULE ONLY:
- Locate the header text that reads: "FOR PERIOD <date1> THRU <date2>"
- Set ReportRunDate = <date2> (the THRU date / later date)
- Do NOT use <date1> (the FOR PERIOD start date)

Set AnchorDate = (ReportRunDate minus 6 calendar days).

Define the 30-day review window as:
- WindowStart = (AnchorDate minus 29 calendar days) = the OLDER / EARLIER date
- WindowEnd = AnchorDate = the NEWER / LATER date
- The window is inclusive of BOTH start and end dates.

Create the output with columns EXACTLY:
Name
Date
Hours
Notes

Extraction rules (do NOT summarize):

1) Employee block detection (must survive page breaks)
- Walk the PDF in reading order from start to end, including across page breaks.
- When you encounter an employee header line, set CurrentName to the employee name as printed (Last, First format).
- The employee header may appear at the end of a line that also contains other header text; still treat the name as the start of a new employee block.
- Treat everything after that name as belonging to CurrentName until the next employee name appears, EVEN if the block continues on the next page.

2) Absence entry detection (IMPORTANT: entries can be packed on one line AND can wrap)
- Do NOT assume one absence entry per line.
- Within each employee block, scan the text token-by-token and extract every occurrence of an absence entry.
- Primary pattern (preferred):
  [EmployeeNumber] [Hours] [Reason] [AbsenceDate]
  - EmployeeNumber is typically 4 digits, but accept 3–5 digits.
  - Hours is a decimal number (accept 1, 1.0, 0.1, 12.0, etc.).
  - Reason is a token like LAT-Early Quit, LAT-Personal, etc.
  - AbsenceDate MUST be the first token after Reason that matches a real calendar date in MM/DD/YYYY format.
  - Capture ONLY a token containing two slashes in MM/DD/YYYY format as AbsenceDate.
  - NEVER use EmployeeNumber, Hours, Total Hours Percentage, page numbers, H-codes, or any other numeric token as the Date.
  - If the token after Reason is not a valid MM/DD/YYYY date, continue scanning forward within the same occurrence until the first valid MM/DD/YYYY date is found; if none is found before the next valid entry pattern, next employee header, or report/footer text, discard that occurrence.
  - A free-text comment MAY appear immediately after the AbsenceDate and may include spaces.
  - Capture the full free-text comment, if present, up to but not including the next valid entry pattern, the next employee header, or report/footer text.
  - Do NOT let comment words interfere with finding the next occurrence.
- Wrapped/continuation recovery rule (to prevent missed entries):
  - Sometimes the PDF wraps so that an entry continues without repeating the EmployeeNumber on the next line.
  - If you see a token sequence that matches:
    [Hours] [Reason] [AbsenceDate]
   and you have a MostRecentEmployeeNumber already seen within the CURRENT employee block, then treat it as a valid entry using that MostRecentEmployeeNumber.
  - In this recovery pattern, AbsenceDate still MUST be a real MM/DD/YYYY token and must never be inferred from any other number.
  - Update MostRecentEmployeeNumber every time you successfully parse a [EmployeeNumber] [Hours] [Reason] [AbsenceDate] pattern.

3) Date capture guardrails (MANDATORY)
- Before writing any output row, validate that Date exactly matches MM/DD/YYYY and parses to a real calendar date.
- If the captured value does not contain two slashes or does not parse as a real date, reject that occurrence and continue scanning.
- Prefer the date token nearest to the Reason within the same occurrence; do not borrow a date from totals, headers, footers, or neighboring entries.
- If multiple numbers appear near an entry, the only value eligible for Date is the valid MM/DD/YYYY token associated with that same occurrence.

4) Exclusions (do not treat as entries)
- Ignore any "Total" / "Total hours worked" / "Overtime hours worked" / "Total Hours Percentage" text and any values that follow those labels unless they are part of a valid entry pattern above.
- Ignore report headers/footers such as "ZATTENDANCE_USWA", "PE1-400 ABSENCE REPORT", page numbers, timestamps, and H### codes unless they are part of a valid entry pattern.

5) Inclusion rules
- Normalize Reason BEFORE any date/window checks using this normalization procedure:
  a) Trim leading/trailing spaces
  b) Collapse any internal multiple spaces to a single space
  c) Treat these as equivalent separators between LAT and Early Quit, and between Early and Quit: "-", " - ", "_", "/" (normalize them all to a single space first, then standardize the final target to "LAT-Early Quit")
  d) Compare case-insensitively
- After normalization, treat ONLY the exact target value "LAT-Early Quit" as a direct reason match.
- Also normalize CommentText, if present, by trimming, collapsing spaces, converting to lowercase, and treating punctuation/separators consistently so explicit phrases like "early quit", "left early", "quit early", "went home early", and "early leave" can be detected reliably.
- Include an extracted occurrence ONLY if:
  AbsenceDate is within WindowStart through WindowEnd (inclusive).
  AND one of the following is true:
    (1) NormalizedReason equals "LAT-Early Quit"
    OR (2) the associated CommentText explicitly states that the employee left early, including phrases such as "left early", "quit early", "went home early", "early leave", or "early quit"
- Entries with comments after the date MUST still be included if they meet the rules above.
- Do NOT include entries whose reason/comment do not explicitly indicate early quitting.
- Discard any occurrence outside the window even if the reason or comment matches.

6) Notes carryforward from prior sheets
- Add the Notes column to the new worksheet even if some rows do not have an existing note.
- After creating the new worksheet, search the three worksheets immediately before it.
- For each row on the new worksheet, look for a matching prior row using the same employee Name and the same Date.
- Search starting with the closest previous tab, then the next closest, then the third closest, and stop at the FIRST populated note found.
- If a matching row is found and the Notes cell on that prior sheet contains a note, copy that most recent prior note into the Notes column on the new sheet.
- If no prior note exists for that Name + Date match, leave the Notes cell blank.

Sanity checks (weekly, no hard-coded examples):
- Confirm every output Date is within WindowStart..WindowEnd (inclusive). If any are outside the window, STOP and fix parsing/window logic.
- Confirm every output Date is an actual MM/DD/YYYY date taken from the entry itself and not any other numeric field. If any row contains a non-date number or unrelated value in the Date column, STOP and fix date parsing logic.
- Confirm every output row is supported by either a Reason that normalizes to "LAT-Early Quit" or a comment that explicitly indicates early quitting. If any other reason appears without a matching early-quit comment, STOP and fix the filter.
- Spot-check accuracy: randomly select at least 3 output rows (or all rows if fewer than 3) and locate each one in the PDF to confirm Name, Date, Hours, and the early-quit basis (Reason and/or CommentText) match, and verify any copied Notes came from the nearest prior sheet with a populated matching note.
- Completeness check: if the PDF contains ANY in-window occurrences that are either coded as "LAT-Early Quit" or have comments explicitly indicating early quitting, the output must not be empty. If output is empty but the PDF contains matches, rerun using token-by-token scanning (not line-based) and ensure continuation recovery is enabled.

7) Output rows
- For every included occurrence, append ONE output row:
  Name  = CurrentName
  Date  = AbsenceDate exactly as printed
  Hours = Hours exactly as printed
  Notes = copied prior-sheet note for the same Name + Date if found; otherwise blank

Materialize Hours as a NUMBER (no formulas, no references).
Do not output any totals rows.
Do not output employees with zero included occurrences.

LAST-RESORT DATE GUARDRAIL (MANDATORY):
After calculating WindowStart and WindowEnd:
- If ANY candidate AbsenceDate is MORE THAN 45 calendar days older than ReportRunDate,
  DISCARD and IGNORE that occurrence regardless of reason/comment.
- If WindowStart resolves to a date MORE THAN 45 calendar days prior to ReportRunDate,
  STOP extraction and use this corrected window instead:
    WindowEnd   = ReportRunDate minus 6 calendar days
    WindowStart = WindowEnd minus 29 calendar days
- Do NOT include any absence date later than WindowEnd under any circumstances.

This guardrail OVERRIDES all other inclusion logic if a conflict is detected.

---

After extraction is complete, format the worksheet:

1. Convert the contiguous data range into an Excel Table.
2. Apply a table style with:
   - NO fill
   - NO banded rows
   - NO banded columns
3. Sort the Table by Date column from HIGHEST to LOWEST.
4. Apply borders to ALL table cells.
5. Bold the header row.
6. Center Columns B and C.
7. Auto-size all columns to fit the text in each cell.

---

After formatting, validate and repair:

1. Confirm the worksheet has EXACTLY four columns: Name, Date, Hours, Notes
2. Confirm all Date values are valid calendar dates
3. Confirm all Hours values are numeric
4. Re-parse the PDF and compare to the worksheet
5. If any entries are missing, APPEND them at the bottom
6. Flag any unmatched entries (in worksheet but not in PDF) but do NOT delete them
7. Report validation results

Do NOT explain during execution - just execute and report the final summary.
